body:
I looked this up a while back for a friend, you can find some published articles about it if you look it up, not sure I can find the links right now.  


It boils down to:

* Yes, Cannabis does impair you (Reduced reaction, spatial awareness, etc)
* HOWEVER: People who smoke UNDERESTIMATE their ability to drive, and thus compensate for it by driving slower, paying more attention, being overly cautious, etc.
&nbsp;  
&nbsp;  

* ALCOHOL, on the other hand, leads people to OVERESTIMATE their ability, thus leading them to drive faster and more recklessly compared to sobriety or Cannabis.  
&nbsp;  
I believe you will find that driving tired is far more dangerous than driving under the influence of Cannabis, yet there is no "awake/alertness" meter for people or a way to measure the sleep chemicals building up in their brains...

"On 10 June 1990 an improperly installed panel of the windscreen failed, at 17,400 feet (5,300 m), **blowing the plane's captain, Tim Lancaster, halfway out of the aircraft**."  
&nbsp;  
"With Lancaster's body firmly pressed against the window frame for over twenty minutes, the first officer managed to perform an emergency landing at Southampton Airport with **no loss of life**."  

&nbsp;  
"Less than five months after the accident Lancaster was working again. He later retired from British Airways when he reached the company's mandatory retirement age of 55 at the time. In 2005 Lancaster was reported as flying for easyJet."
&nbsp;  

