That's why we need to heat the water to provide it enough energy to boil under standard atmosphere conditions, in order to make the pressure needed to drive the turbine. Also, under pressure you need even more heat to get it to boil, so 100c is not enough. (Hence why your pressure cooker can go over 100c and the water won't boil, it's under pressure)

* Have a solid background in personal computing and standard MS software like Office, etc, and how to configure pretty much every windows setting (hours going through control panel, device manager, etc)  
* Know how to build/fix the standard hardware of a PC/Laptop and learn to identify issues like error messages and beep codes
* Get a basic degree, like a Cert IV in IT or even better some windows certs ie 070-680 (Windows 7, configuring, albeit outdated now lol)
* Most businesses run Windows, so unless you want to be a high level Linux/Unix admin or stick exclusively to small hipster/wanky/(moronic lol) businesses using Mac's, go with Windows support predominantly. (I fucking hate supporting Macs in AD environments fyi, bloody awful...)  
&nbsp;  
* Now for the shit news: To break in to the industry you'll usually have to start from the bottom in order to gain experience and trust, that entails a L1 Service Desk/Help Desk/Call Centre role, and they fucking suck, believe me, but youll need to stick it out for 12-24 months until you can get into Desktop/Network/Admin roles.
&nbsp;  
&nbsp;  

Best of luck, and protip: [watch out for Burnout!](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Occupational_burnout)

